@title Message queue for order events
@subtitle Decision record for the Orders team
@author Lina Okafor, Staff Engineer
@date 2026-10-12

We need a queue between checkout and fulfilment that survives a regional outage, handles 4,000 messages a second at peak, and does not need a dedicated team to run it.

## Options

table = Option,Monthly cost,Peak throughput,Replay,Ops load; **Amazon SQS**,"$1,150",Unlimited,No,Low; Kafka (MSK),"$6,400","50,000/s",Yes,High; RabbitMQ (Amazon MQ),"$2,300","8,000/s",No,Medium; Google Pub/Sub,"$1,480",Unlimited,Yes (7 days),Low

## Pros and cons

### Amazon SQS
- Pro: fully managed, nothing to operate
- Pro: scales automatically past peak
- Con: no replay of consumed messages
- Con: 256 KB message limit

### Kafka (MSK)
- Pro: replay up to retention
- Pro: supports stream processing later
- Con: needs partition planning
- Con: we own broker upgrades

### RabbitMQ (Amazon MQ)
- Pro: flexible routing
- Con: we would own capacity planning
- Con: lowest throughput ceiling

## Recommendation

! **Choose Amazon SQS.** It covers peak load with no capacity planning, and replay can be added later with an S3 archive of every message.

> Pick the queue you never have to think about, then spend the saved time on the consumers.
> — Lina Okafor
